{
  "platform_id": "bXwLmZBa0Lo",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:bXwLmZBa0Lo",
    "purpose": "A live AMA where Kenji answers viewer questions about woks and wok cooking: materials, seasoning, maintenance, burners and induction, accessories, oils, technique and troubleshooting stir-fries.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "A carbon steel wok, flat-bottomed for home burners, works best when its heat is concentrated at the bottom and it is cared for simply. Good stir-fries come from managing heat, moisture and batch size rather than from building heavy seasoning or browning.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Wok cooking wants concentrated bottom heat and uneven heat zones, unlike western clad pans such as the Titanium Pro, which use aluminum to spread heat evenly.",
      "Kenji recommends carbon steel woks. The Our Place wok he designed is super nitrided for hardness, slickness and rust resistance, but a $40 to $60 raw carbon steel wok is fine for beginners.",
      "Wok seasoning is mainly the initial conversion of raw carbon steel to black oxide, not building polymer layers like cast iron.",
      "After cooking: rinse, dry the wok on the burner, then rub in a very thin film of oil. Small rust spots can simply be washed off.",
      "Kenji preheats with a thin rubbed-in layer of oil as a smoke-point temperature gauge, then adds fresh cooking oil.",
      "Induction handles most wok dishes if you toss less and use the spatula more. True wok hei needs a live flame, though a blowtorch can approximate it. Liquid smoke cannot imitate it.",
      "On induction, the fast/medium/slow power settings are the analogue of gas heat levels, because heat flux matters more than set temperature.",
      "For indoor burners use a flat-bottom wok without a wok ring. A portable butane burner of 12,500 BTU or more can outperform a standard western burner.",
      "Cook in small batches of about half a pound, using the landing bowl method, to prevent steaming. A strainer, a slicker wok and a starch slurry to emulsify the sauce prevent oily stir-fries."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro",
        "summary": "Kenji opens the second live AMA, focused on woks, and mentions the wok he designed with Our Place.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 0
      },
      {
        "title": "Carbon steel vs. titanium",
        "summary": "Explains how western burners and clad pans (All-Clad, Our Place Titanium Pro) spread heat evenly, while woks need concentrated bottom heat. Covers why carbon steel is the best wok material, what super nitriding is, and price options including a Yoshikawa raw carbon steel wok. Includes asides on diamond conduction and a copper pan.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Initial seasoning",
        "summary": "Contrasts cast iron polymer seasoning with wok seasoning, which is converting raw carbon steel to black oxide. Covers how to do it on gas, in the oven, on induction or with a torch, and notes pre-seasoned woks.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Making it non-stick",
        "summary": "Explains the restaurant two-oil method and Kenji's home version: a rubbed-in thin oil layer used as a smoke-point gauge, then fresh cooking oil. Also covers why not to use butter.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 24
      },
      {
        "title": "Reseasoning with oil",
        "summary": "Post-cooking routine: rinse, dry on the burner, and wipe in a very thin coat of oil to prevent rust without leaving a gummy residue.",
        "first_segment": 24,
        "last_segment": 27
      },
      {
        "title": "Rusty woks",
        "summary": "How to handle rust spots, patina and rough buildup, when a wok is beyond saving, and using a bamboo wok brush.",
        "first_segment": 28,
        "last_segment": 31
      },
      {
        "title": "Induction cooking",
        "summary": "Covers the components of wok hei and why it is hard to get on induction. Advises tossing less and using the spatula more on induction, and using a blowtorch for smokiness.",
        "first_segment": 31,
        "last_segment": 36
      },
      {
        "title": "Wok hei flavor",
        "summary": "Why liquid smoke cannot imitate wok hei: liquid smoke is condensed smoldering wood smoke, while wok hei is combusted oil.",
        "first_segment": 37,
        "last_segment": 39
      },
      {
        "title": "Sticky sides vs non-stick center",
        "summary": "Western burners heat the sides of a wok, burning on sauce. Remedies include the WokMon, removing the burner diffuser, outdoor burners, or a 12,500+ BTU butane burner, plus ventilation advice.",
        "first_segment": 39,
        "last_segment": 43
      },
      {
        "title": "Induction temperature",
        "summary": "Explains temperature-controlled induction versus heat flux on gas. Recommends maximum or fast settings on burners like the Breville Control Freak.",
        "first_segment": 44,
        "last_segment": 47
      },
      {
        "title": "Wok rings",
        "summary": "What wok rings are for, why they reduce heat on home burners, and why Kenji recommends flat-bottom woks indoors and round-bottom woks only on outdoor burners.",
        "first_segment": 47,
        "last_segment": 50
      },
      {
        "title": "Which oil for cooking",
        "summary": "Coconut oil and the range of neutral high smoke point oils, plus specialty oils such as Sichuan roasted rapeseed oil (caiziyou) and roasted sesame oil.",
        "first_segment": 50,
        "last_segment": 52
      },
      {
        "title": "Flat vs dome lids",
        "summary": "Dome, wooden and fitted glass wok lids for steaming and smoking. Kenji says performance does not differ between the lid styles.",
        "first_segment": 52,
        "last_segment": 54
      },
      {
        "title": "Building up carbon",
        "summary": "Says you should not build up polymer or carbon layers, and advises against Bar Keepers Friend on woks.",
        "first_segment": 55,
        "last_segment": 57
      },
      {
        "title": "Tossing food wok-through",
        "summary": "Demonstrates the four-part wok toss with raw rice, which hand to use, spatula and ladle techniques, and two-handed tossing for heavy loads.",
        "first_segment": 57,
        "last_segment": 62
      },
      {
        "title": "Order for adding food",
        "summary": "The general stir-fry workflow: prepped bowls in order, a landing pad bowl, cooking in batches, why wok cooking avoids browning, adding sauce around the sides, and adjusting sauce consistency.",
        "first_segment": 62,
        "last_segment": 72
      },
      {
        "title": "Steamed vs. fried",
        "summary": "Food steams when the burner is too weak or diffuses heat badly. The fix is smaller batches and better tossing.",
        "first_segment": 72,
        "last_segment": 74
      },
      {
        "title": "Oily stir-fries",
        "summary": "Avoiding greasy stir-fries with a slick wok, a strainer in the landing pad and emulsifying the sauce with starch slurry. Mapo tofu is noted as an intentional exception.",
        "first_segment": 74,
        "last_segment": 76
      },
      {
        "title": "Outro",
        "summary": "Sign-off and plugs for Kenji's website, Patreon, shop, books and live events.",
        "first_segment": 76,
        "last_segment": 77
      }
    ],
